  <h2>How to pick</h2>
  <p>If you want a seat you can book, pay for safely and get refunded on if the ride falls through, use a marketplace with verification and payment protection. If you are comfortable arranging cash with a stranger and vetting them yourself, the boards are free to use and cover routes no app does yet. On Seat Sherpa you can also post a request on the <a href="/requests">ride request board</a> when nothing is listed; drivers check it before deciding what to post.</p>

  <div class="callout" style="text-align:center">
    <h3>See live seat prices on your route</h3>
    <p style="margin-bottom:6px">Drivers post trips they are already taking. Book in the browser or the app; no account needed to look.</p>
    <a class="btn" href="/rides?c=compare">Browse rides →</a>
    <p style="font-size:14px;color:#8a8a92;margin-top:10px">Driving somewhere yourself? <a href="/post">Post the ride</a> and split the cost.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <div class="faq"><h3>What is the best carpool app for San Francisco to Los Angeles?</h3><p>Seat Sherpa is the booked-ahead carpool marketplace for California and Nevada, with verified drivers, payment held until pickup and seats capped at $55. Craigslist rideshare and Facebook groups also list the route but have no verification or payment protection.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Is there a BlaBlaCar in California?</h3><p>No. BlaBlaCar has never operated in the United States. Seat Sherpa fills the same role in California and Nevada: drivers post intercity trips they are already taking and passengers share the cost.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Is Waze Carpool still available?</h3><p>No. Google shut down Waze Carpool in 2022. For daily commutes, employer or agency commute programs remain; for city-to-city trips in California and Nevada, Seat Sherpa is the booked-ahead option.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Does Hitch work in California?</h3><p>No. Hitch operates in Texas and neighbouring states. Seat Sherpa covers the California and Nevada corridors.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>How much does a Seat Sherpa seat cost?</h3><p>It depends on the route and the driver, but every seat has a hard cap: the IRS standard mileage rate (about $0.76 per mile in 2026) multiplied by the drivable miles, divided across the seats, and never more than $55 a seat. Riders pay the seat price plus a 5% service fee, shown before you pay. Live prices for each corridor are on the route pages and in the ride browser.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Is Seat Sherpa safe?</h3><p>Every driver verifies a phone number by SMS, uploads a driver’s license that is checked for expiry, registers the vehicle, and connects a Stripe account that verifies identity and bank details. Riders see the driver’s photo, trip count and reviews before booking, can filter for women drivers, and can block anyone. A 4-digit pickup code confirms the rider got in the car and is what releases payment.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>What is the cancellation policy?</h3><p>One standard policy on every ride: cancel 24 or more hours before departure for a full seat refund, 2 to 24 hours before for 50%, and inside 2 hours there is no refund. The 5% service fee is non-refundable when the rider cancels. If the driver cancels, the rider gets 100% back including the fee.</p></div>

